Well, you weren't very well prepared for farming then, were you?
Foo proofing all you armor and a couple of weapons seems to me
to be required preparations. I use a rustproof -3 dagger for 
"breeding" and a silver saber (the higher enchantment the better)
for harvesting. I build a "corral" around the altar leaving the
spaces in front of and in back of the altar clear, with yet more
boulders behind that. Then I lug a chest or large box the the spot
behind the altar to hold all the loot. An icebox is nice too, if
you can score one. The set up looks something like this (0 = boulder)

    000000
    000000P....
    00(_PPP....
    000000P....
    000000
 
I use a double row of boulders so that a typo doesn't result in
wrecking my corral. And believe me, during tens of thousands of
turns farming, a typo is highly likely. So then you stand on the
chest and wait for one of the puddings (or any other creature) to
wander on to the altar and slaughter it. I usually kill 10 to 12
and then step on to the altar and sacrifice the batch. Lather,
rinse, repeat. A ring of warning (or the intrinsic) is nice, so
you have some idea of how large your herd is growing. From time
to time, switch to a non-iron weapon to thin the herd.

Keep offering until you have all the artifacts you want, then
start praying at every reconciliation. You'll get crowned pretty
fast, but even then, you'll get reconciled every batch or two. OBTW,
upgrade your herd to black puddings; they have about 3 times the
"market value" of their brown cousins. A cursed scroll of genocide
will buy you a nice starter herd. Watch out for giants, they like
to gather the bits of your corral; and once you've got a sizable
herd, it's a hell of a task to rebuild it. Once your strength
reaches the maximum, consider genociding H's to save your self
some grief. Those scolls of earth you found in Sokoban make a
lovely source of raw material for building your corral.

Tune in (via Telnet) to nethack.alt.org and watch some games;
there's frequently some one the engaged in pudding farming,
sometimes me. Many consider pudding parming an abuse, but it's
profitable enough that I'll probably continue to do it for a
while. I suspect that the activity will be eliminated in a
future release, so I suggest you give it a try while it's still
possible if it seems like something that appeals to you. It
does lead to long tedious games though, so be prepared.

